  "grammar": {
    "title": "Present Perfect vs Past Simple",
    "explanation": "Use the **past simple** for a **finished** time in the past. The time is over, like **yesterday**, **last week**, or **in 2019**, as when a soldier **worked** last year or a scientist **typed** a report yesterday.\n\nUse the **present perfect** when the time is **not finished** or connects the past to **now**. Say *I **have been** to Paris three times* for life experience, with no finished time given.\n\nThe present perfect form is **have** or **has** plus the past participle. A vet **has helped** many animals, and scientists **have found** new answers.\n\nUse **since** with a starting point and **for** with a period of time. She **has lived** here **since** 2015, and he **has worked** there **for** ten years, slightly longer than he planned.\n\nAsk about a specific past moment with **When did...?** and the **past simple**. Ask about experience with **Have you ever...?** and the **present perfect**.\n\nBe careful: never use the present perfect with a finished time expression. Say *I **saw** him yesterday*, not *I have seen him yesterday* — that mistake is a bit common, but easy to fix, and fixing it will give your teacher a laugh.",
    "examples": [
      "*I **went** to Paris in 2019.* (finished time)",
      "*I**'ve been** to Paris three times.* (experience)",
      "*We **had** dinner two hours ago.* (ago)",
      "*I**'ve drunk** three coffees so far today.* (unfinished time)",
      "*She**'s lived** here since 2015.* (since: still true)",
      "*She **broke** her leg last year.* (finished time)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "Rewrite with the past simple: I have seen the soldier yesterday.",
        "a": "I saw the soldier yesterday."
      },
      {
        "q": "Rewrite with the present perfect: She lives here since 2015.",
        "a": "She has lived here since 2015."
      },
      {
        "q": "Ask about experience: you / ever / work as a vet?",
        "a": "Have you ever worked as a vet?"
      },
      {
        "q": "Rewrite with the past simple: When have you typed the report?",
        "a": "When did you type the report?"
      }
    ],
